WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.215
Lesson 1.8.

00:00:02.215 --> 00:00:04.560
Privacy and physical security.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.235
By the end, you'll know how to

00:00:06.235 --> 00:00:08.773
keep your holdings private, and your household

00:00:08.773 --> 00:00:10.340
safe, as what you hold grows.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:12.930
Here's the plan.

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:16.865
Why blockchains being public matters more than people expect.

00:00:16.865 --> 00:00:19.400
How to actually link less, in practice.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.733
The physical risk that only shows up once

00:00:21.733 --> 00:00:24.700
holdings are large enough to be worth targeting.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:26.350
And the home and device habits

00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:27.575
that go with all of it.

00:00:28.875 --> 00:00:30.105
Public by default.

00:00:31.405 --> 00:00:33.981
Here's exactly what happens once one address

00:00:33.981 --> 00:00:36.170
gets linked to you, step by step.

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:39.312
Someone connects your name to an address, maybe from

00:00:39.312 --> 00:00:42.280
a screenshot, a withdrawal, or a public post.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:44.250
From that moment, its full balance

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:46.295
is visible to anyone who looks.

00:00:46.295 --> 00:00:49.957
So is its complete history: every deposit, every trade,

00:00:49.957 --> 00:00:54.245
every counterparty, going back to the very first transaction.

00:00:54.245 --> 00:00:56.330
And that visibility doesn't fade.

00:00:56.330 --> 00:00:59.000
It's permanent, and it covers every future

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:01.173
transaction too, not just the past.

00:01:02.473 --> 00:01:05.040
So here's the actual contrast worth holding

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:06.798
in your head, day to day.

00:01:06.798 --> 00:01:09.652
A traditional bank balance is private by default;

00:01:09.652 --> 00:01:11.608
only you and the bank see it.

00:01:11.608 --> 00:01:14.283
A blockchain address is public by default;

00:01:14.283 --> 00:01:16.461
only the link between you and that

00:01:16.461 --> 00:01:18.825
address is ever hidden, and once that

00:01:18.825 --> 00:01:20.878
link is made, it can't be unmade.

00:01:22.178 --> 00:01:24.299
Worth auditing your own exposure right

00:01:24.299 --> 00:01:26.393
now, before assuming you're fine.

00:01:26.393 --> 00:01:28.496
Search your own old posts and forum

00:01:28.496 --> 00:01:31.308
history for any address you've ever pasted.

00:01:31.308 --> 00:01:33.817
Check whether any address you use has

00:01:33.817 --> 00:01:37.088
an E.N.S.-style name pointing at it publicly.

00:01:37.088 --> 00:01:39.876
And check whether you've ever linked a wallet to

00:01:39.876 --> 00:01:42.793
a public profile, a Discord, or a leaderboard.

00:01:42.793 --> 00:01:45.429
Whatever turns up, you now know exactly what's

00:01:45.429 --> 00:01:48.176
already exposed, and can start a clean, separate

00:01:48.176 --> 00:01:50.868
address for anything that matters going forward.

00:01:52.168 --> 00:01:54.101
If your own audit just turned up

00:01:54.101 --> 00:01:56.795
an address you've already exposed, here's the

00:01:56.795 --> 00:01:58.793
actual fix, not just a worry.

00:01:58.793 --> 00:02:01.308
Treat that address as burned for privacy

00:02:01.308 --> 00:02:04.553
purposes, permanently; the link can't be undone.

00:02:04.553 --> 00:02:08.337
Move meaningful holdings to a fresh, never-shared address, using

00:02:08.337 --> 00:02:11.373
a method that doesn't itself create a new link.

00:02:11.373 --> 00:02:13.317
And simply stop using the old one

00:02:13.317 --> 00:02:15.718
for anything beyond what's already public.

00:02:17.018 --> 00:02:18.108
Quick check.

00:02:18.108 --> 00:02:20.633
Once an address is linked to your identity,

00:02:20.633 --> 00:02:22.813
what can anyone see, and for how long?

00:02:27.233 --> 00:02:30.416
The answer: its full balance and complete history,

00:02:30.416 --> 00:02:34.535
permanently, including every future transaction from that address.

00:02:35.835 --> 00:02:36.690
Link less.

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:40.492
Here's what “link less” actually looks like

00:02:40.492 --> 00:02:42.555
as a habit, in order of impact.

00:02:42.555 --> 00:02:44.641
Never post an address, a balance,

00:02:44.641 --> 00:02:47.395
or a win publicly, screenshots included.

00:02:47.395 --> 00:02:50.482
Use one address for public activity, donations,

00:02:50.482 --> 00:02:53.054
NFTs, things you don't mind being seen,

00:02:53.054 --> 00:02:56.055
and a completely separate one for savings.

00:02:56.055 --> 00:02:59.348
Know that an exchange withdrawal links your verified

00:02:59.348 --> 00:03:03.065
identity to whichever address receives it, permanently.

00:03:03.065 --> 00:03:05.592
And think twice before using an ENS-style

00:03:05.592 --> 00:03:08.600
name service on your savings address specifically.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:12.585
Here's exactly why an exchange withdrawal is

00:03:12.585 --> 00:03:14.735
the one people miss most often.

00:03:14.735 --> 00:03:17.291
You complete K.Y.C. on an exchange,

00:03:17.291 --> 00:03:20.005
which verifies your real identity.

00:03:20.005 --> 00:03:21.740
You withdraw to an address.

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:25.003
That withdrawal is now a permanent record connecting

00:03:25.003 --> 00:03:28.512
your verified identity to that specific address, sitting

00:03:28.512 --> 00:03:32.260
on the exchange's books and, in principle, discoverable.

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:34.999
From that point on, that address is not anonymous

00:03:34.999 --> 00:03:37.245
to anyone who could ever see that record.

00:03:38.545 --> 00:03:41.053
And here's exactly why a name service cuts

00:03:41.053 --> 00:03:43.570
the other way from what people expect.

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:46.012
It exists to make an address memorable

00:03:46.012 --> 00:03:48.329
and easy to find, which is genuinely

00:03:48.329 --> 00:03:50.820
useful for a public-facing address.

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:54.398
On a savings address specifically, that convenience becomes

00:03:54.398 --> 00:03:56.903
the problem: it makes the one address you

00:03:56.903 --> 00:03:59.587
most want private the easiest one for anyone

00:03:59.587 --> 00:04:01.615
to look up and associate with you.

00:04:02.915 --> 00:04:05.997
Here's how a chain-analysis effort actually builds a

00:04:05.997 --> 00:04:09.030
full profile, since it's rarely one clue alone.

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:11.761
It starts with an exchange withdrawal record,

00:04:11.761 --> 00:04:14.791
linking your verified identity to one address.

00:04:14.791 --> 00:04:17.100
From there, address reuse links that address

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:19.196
to others you've used the same way.

00:04:19.196 --> 00:04:21.216
A public post, or an ENS name,

00:04:21.216 --> 00:04:23.796
adds a public label to the cluster.

00:04:23.796 --> 00:04:26.511
And the combination, not any single piece,

00:04:26.511 --> 00:04:29.857
produces a complete profile: identity, full balance,

00:04:29.857 --> 00:04:31.941
and behaviour, all tied together.

00:04:33.241 --> 00:04:34.331
Quick check.

00:04:34.331 --> 00:04:36.765
Why does an exchange withdrawal specifically

00:04:36.765 --> 00:04:38.550
link your identity to an address?

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:46.614
The answer: because the exchange verified your identity

00:04:46.614 --> 00:04:49.932
through KYC, and its records connect that identity

00:04:49.932 --> 00:04:52.340
to whichever address you withdrew to.

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:54.625
Physical risk.

00:04:55.925 --> 00:04:59.180
Here's the plain, uncomfortable version of this risk.

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:02.595
Criminals have targeted people known, or believed, to

00:05:02.595 --> 00:05:06.390
hold significant crypto, specifically because a private key

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:09.236
can move everything in minutes, with no bank

00:05:09.236 --> 00:05:11.939
to call and no transaction to reverse.

00:05:11.939 --> 00:05:14.484
This isn't a hypothetical for this lesson;

00:05:14.484 --> 00:05:16.970
it's documented, and it's exactly why the

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:19.634
earlier habit of never posting your holdings

00:05:19.634 --> 00:05:22.179
matters as much as any technical safeguard.

00:05:23.479 --> 00:05:27.374
So here's how multisig actually defeats coercion specifically,

00:05:27.374 --> 00:05:30.094
step by step, since it's the one structural

00:05:30.094 --> 00:05:33.114
defence that works even under direct threat.

00:05:33.114 --> 00:05:35.156
Your funds require, say, two out of

00:05:35.156 --> 00:05:37.044
three keys to move, not one.

00:05:37.044 --> 00:05:39.615
Those keys are held in separate physical

00:05:39.615 --> 00:05:42.799
locations, ideally by separate trusted people.

00:05:42.799 --> 00:05:45.163
If someone coerces you specifically, you

00:05:45.163 --> 00:05:47.539
alone cannot produce the second key.

00:05:47.539 --> 00:05:49.368
And that means there is nothing a

00:05:49.368 --> 00:05:51.789
threat against you alone can actually unlock.

00:05:53.089 --> 00:05:55.789
Put a single key and multisig side by side,

00:05:55.789 --> 00:05:59.464
under exactly this threat, and the difference is total.

00:05:59.464 --> 00:06:02.590
A single key means one person, coerced once, can

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:06.654
move everything, immediately, with no second step required.

00:06:06.654 --> 00:06:09.820
Two-of-three multisig means one person, even under

00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:12.923
direct threat, simply cannot move anything alone,

00:06:12.923 --> 00:06:15.344
no matter how much pressure is applied.

00:06:16.644 --> 00:06:19.709
One sobering, dated fact behind this chapter.

00:06:19.709 --> 00:06:25.063
Crypto-crime researchers and journalists have documented dozens of publicised

00:06:25.063 --> 00:06:29.662
physical robbery and coercion cases targeting known crypto holders

00:06:29.662 --> 00:06:33.119
across recent years, worldwide, not a handful.

00:06:33.119 --> 00:06:36.239
This is outside research, not this program's own

00:06:36.239 --> 00:06:39.295
data, but the pattern is consistent enough that

00:06:39.295 --> 00:06:42.606
low profile and multisig aren't optional extras for

00:06:42.606 --> 00:06:44.834
anyone holding a meaningful amount.

00:06:46.134 --> 00:06:49.247
Discretion doesn't mean total secrecy from everyone,

00:06:49.247 --> 00:06:51.544
so here's the honest line to draw.

00:06:51.544 --> 00:06:54.304
Worth telling: whoever holds one of your

00:06:54.304 --> 00:06:57.400
multisig keys, and, for real estate planning,

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:00.362
a lawyer bound by confidentiality who knows

00:07:00.362 --> 00:07:03.489
a plan exists, not necessarily the amount.

00:07:03.489 --> 00:07:07.893
Not worth telling: casual acquaintances, coworkers, or anyone with

00:07:07.893 --> 00:07:11.114
no structural role in accessing or recovering it.

00:07:12.414 --> 00:07:13.504
Quick check.

00:07:13.504 --> 00:07:17.012
Why does 2-of-3 multisig specifically protect against a

00:07:17.012 --> 00:07:19.769
coercion threat, where a single key doesn't?

00:07:24.189 --> 00:07:26.826
The answer: no single coerced person can

00:07:26.826 --> 00:07:29.270
produce the second key, so nobody can

00:07:29.270 --> 00:07:31.649
be forced, alone, to move everything.

00:07:32.949 --> 00:07:34.184
Home and devices.

00:07:35.484 --> 00:07:37.304
Here's the home and device side,

00:07:37.304 --> 00:07:39.369
as a straightforward habit list.

00:07:39.369 --> 00:07:42.447
Hardware wallets and backups stay physically out of

00:07:42.447 --> 00:07:45.765
sight, never displayed or left visible to visitors.

00:07:45.765 --> 00:07:49.118
Devices holding any wallet access stay encrypted,

00:07:49.118 --> 00:07:52.100
with a real passcode, not left unlocked.

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:55.127
And holdings simply aren't discussed with people

00:07:55.127 --> 00:07:57.413
who have no need to know, including,

00:07:57.413 --> 00:08:00.378
quietly, most extended family and acquaintances.

00:08:01.678 --> 00:08:04.507
And before that first large deposit specifically,

00:08:04.507 --> 00:08:06.618
do these three things, in order.

00:08:06.618 --> 00:08:09.315
Set up a dedicated savings address, separate

00:08:09.315 --> 00:08:12.013
from anything you've ever used publicly.

00:08:12.013 --> 00:08:15.408
Leave it unnamed; no ENS, no label anywhere.

00:08:15.408 --> 00:08:17.704
And if you tell anyone at all that it

00:08:17.704 --> 00:08:21.270
exists, tell one trusted person, through a secure channel,

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:24.533
not a casual conversation where others might overhear.

00:08:25.833 --> 00:08:28.083
Alex posts a screenshot of a big

00:08:28.083 --> 00:08:30.973
win, with the address clearly visible.

00:08:30.973 --> 00:08:34.502
From that single post, anyone can now see Alex's

00:08:34.502 --> 00:08:38.548
full balance, and follow every future move, forever.

00:08:38.548 --> 00:08:40.493
A scammer or thief now has a

00:08:40.493 --> 00:08:43.408
specific, verified target, not a guess.

00:08:43.408 --> 00:08:47.216
The better version: no screenshots ever, a vault

00:08:47.216 --> 00:08:50.479
address that has never been shared, and a

00:08:50.479 --> 00:08:53.976
2-of-3 multisig, so no single key holder can

00:08:53.976 --> 00:08:56.618
be coerced into draining it alone.

00:08:57.918 --> 00:08:59.378
Here's your checklist.

00:08:59.378 --> 00:09:00.913
Do it now, for real.

00:09:00.913 --> 00:09:03.178
Your savings addresses have never been

00:09:03.178 --> 00:09:05.568
posted or linked publicly, anywhere.

00:09:05.568 --> 00:09:07.743
No holdings screenshots or wins

00:09:07.743 --> 00:09:09.743
go on social media, ever.

00:09:09.743 --> 00:09:11.562
Vault keys are held so that no

00:09:11.562 --> 00:09:14.208
single person can move everything alone.

00:09:14.208 --> 00:09:16.539
And backups are stored discreetly, somewhere

00:09:16.539 --> 00:09:17.938
nobody would think to look.

00:09:19.238 --> 00:09:20.483
Let's recap.

00:09:20.483 --> 00:09:23.758
Blockchains are public by default, permanently, and only

00:09:23.758 --> 00:09:26.413
the link to your identity is ever hidden.

00:09:26.413 --> 00:09:30.256
Linking less is a practical habit: separate addresses, care

00:09:30.256 --> 00:09:34.847
around exchange withdrawals, caution with name services on savings.

00:09:34.847 --> 00:09:37.279
Physical risk is real once holdings are

00:09:37.279 --> 00:09:40.198
worth targeting, and multisig is the structural

00:09:40.198 --> 00:09:43.112
defence that actually works under coercion.

00:09:43.112 --> 00:09:46.346
And ordinary discretion, at home and on your devices,

00:09:46.346 --> 00:09:48.802
is what keeps all of it holding together.

00:09:50.102 --> 00:09:53.470
Stay quiet about what you hold, separate your addresses

00:09:53.470 --> 00:09:56.658
by purpose, and structure real holdings so no single

00:09:56.658 --> 00:10:00.687
person, including you under threat, can move everything alone.

00:10:00.687 --> 00:10:05.012
Next up, Lesson 1.9: Smart accounts and account abstraction.
